{"id": "agentic-tool-use-001", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- search_docs(query: str) -> list of {doc_id, title, snippet}\n- get_doc(doc_id: str) -> full text\n- create_ticket(title: str, body: str, priority: str) -> ticket_id\n- notify(channel: str, message: str)\n\nTask: Find the company's VPN troubleshooting guide and file a P2 ticket titled 'VPN drops every 20 minutes' whose body quotes the guide's first recommended step.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) Must search_docs first (query about VPN troubleshooting), then get_doc on the best hit to obtain the actual first recommended step BEFORE creating the ticket — quoting requires the doc content, so get_doc must precede create_ticket; (2) ticket priority 'P2', title exactly as given; (3) should not create the ticket from the snippet alone (snippet may not contain the step); (4) notify is optional/not required. General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "agentic-tool-use-002", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- get_user(user_id: str) -> {name, email, plan, status}\n- list_orders(user_id: str, limit: int) -> [{order_id, total, status, date}]\n- issue_refund(order_id: str, amount: float, reason: str) -> refund_id\n- send_email(to: str, subject: str, body: str)\n\nTask: User u_4821 says their most recent order arrived damaged and wants a refund. Refund the full amount of their most recent order and email them a confirmation that includes the refund ID.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) list_orders(u_4821) to identify the most recent order — must NOT guess an order_id; (2) issue_refund with that order's exact total as amount; (3) send_email must come after issue_refund because the confirmation must include the refund_id; (4) get_user is optional (email may come from context) but using it for the address is acceptable; (5) refund amount must match the order total, not a made-up number. General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "agentic-tool-use-003", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- get_metrics(service: str, window: str) -> {error_rate, p99_latency, request_count}\n- get_deploys(service: str, limit: int) -> [{deploy_id, time, author, change_summary}]\n- rollback(service: str, deploy_id: str) -> status\n- page_oncall(service: str, message: str)\n\nTask: The Atlas search service is paging. Determine whether a recent deploy is the likely cause; if a deploy in the last 2 hours correlates with elevated errors, roll it back and page the on-call with a summary. If no recent deploy correlates, just page on-call with the metrics — do not roll back.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) Calls get_metrics and get_deploys (may be parallel — independent); (2) decision logic: rollback ONLY if a deploy within the last 2 hours correlates with the elevated error rate; (3) rollback targets that specific deploy_id, not a guessed one; (4) page_oncall happens in both branches with a summary; (5) must explicitly state the conditional branch, not blindly roll back. General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "agentic-tool-use-004", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- read_file(path: str) -> content\n- write_file(path: str, content: str) -> status\n- run_tests(suite: str) -> {passed, failed, failures: [names]}\n- git_commit(message: str) -> commit_hash\n\nTask: Fix the failing test 'test_parse_empty_input' in the parser suite: inspect the parser source, make a minimal fix, verify the whole suite passes, then commit.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) run_tests or read_file first to inspect — must read the relevant parser file before editing; (2) write_file with a minimal targeted change; (3) run_tests again AFTER the fix to verify (full suite, not just the one test); (4) git_commit only after tests pass; (5) should state what to do if tests still fail (iterate, not commit). General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "agentic-tool-use-005", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- check_inventory(sku: str) -> {on_hand, reorder_threshold, supplier_id}\n- create_purchase_order(supplier_id: str, items: [{sku, qty}]) -> po_id\n- get_sales_velocity(sku: str, days: int) -> units_per_day\n- set_reorder_alert(sku: str, threshold: int)\n\nTask: For SKU PLT-2081, decide whether to reorder. Policy: reorder 30 days of stock if on_hand is below the reorder threshold. Email-free flow — just create the purchase order if needed and say why or why not.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) check_inventory first to compare on_hand vs reorder_threshold; (2) if reordering, get_sales_velocity(sku, 30) to size the order (qty = ~30 x velocity) — must NOT invent a quantity; (3) create_purchase_order uses the supplier_id returned by check_inventory; (4) if on_hand >= threshold, no PO — must explain; (5) set_reorder_alert is optional and not required by the policy. General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "agentic-tool-use-006", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- calendar_find_conflicts(user: str, date: str) -> [{start, end, title}]\n- calendar_create(user: str, start: str, end: str, title: str, attendees: [str]) -> event_id\n- room_search(capacity: int, date: str, start: str, end: str) -> [room names]\n- room_book(room: str, start: str, end: str) -> booking_id\n\nTask: Schedule a 1-hour design review for user k.tate on 2026-08-20, any time between 13:00 and 17:00, in a room that seats 8. Pick the earliest conflict-free slot and book the room.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) calendar_find_conflicts for that date first; (2) derive the earliest 1-hour gap within 13:00-17:00 from the returned conflicts — must reason over actual conflict data, not assume 13:00 is free; (3) room_search and room_book should use the SAME chosen slot; (4) calendar_create only after the slot is determined; (5) room_book may need to precede or follow calendar_create but must reference the same time; (6) capacity argument = 8. General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "agentic-tool-use-007", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- db_query(sql: str) -> rows\n- chart_render(series: [{label, values}], kind: str) -> chart_url\n- post_message(channel: str, text: str, attachments: [str])\n\nTask: Post to #weekly-metrics a bar chart of new signups per day for the last 7 days, with a one-line caption naming the peak day.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) db_query with a SQL query grouping signups by day over a 7-day window — the SQL must be plausible (GROUP BY date, WHERE created_at within last 7 days); (2) chart_render AFTER the query, kind 'bar', series built from the query rows — chart cannot precede data; (3) caption must name the actual peak day derived from the data, not a guess; (4) post_message last with the chart_url attached. General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "agentic-tool-use-008", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- translate_text(text: str, target_lang: str) -> translated text\n- detect_language(text: str) -> language code\n- glossary_lookup(term: str, lang: str) -> preferred translation or null\n- save_translation(doc_id: str, lang: str, content: str)\n\nTask: Translate document doc_77's abstract into German for saving under the same doc ID. The abstract uses the term 'onboarding', which must follow the glossary if a German preferred term exists; otherwise keep 'Onboarding' untranslated.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) glossary_lookup('onboarding', 'de') should happen (before or alongside translation) to determine the preferred term; (2) translate_text with target_lang 'de', applying the glossary term if found / leaving 'Onboarding' untranslated otherwise — must express this conditional explicitly; (3) save_translation('doc_77', 'de', ...) with the final text; (4) detect_language is unnecessary since source/target are known — using it is a minor waste, not an error; (5) save must come after translation. General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "agentic-tool-use-009", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- fetch_url(url: str) -> page text\n- extract_table(page_text: str, table_index: int) -> rows\n- currency_convert(amount: float, from_ccy: str, to_ccy: str, date: str) -> converted amount\n- append_row(sheet_id: str, row: list)\n\nTask: From the fictional supplier price list at https://prices.nordwind-supply.example/list, pull the first table, find the unit price of 'Copper rivets 4mm' (listed in EUR), convert it to USD using today's rate, and append [item name, EUR price, USD price] to sheet sh_118.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) fetch_url -> extract_table(text, 0) in that order; (2) locate the 'Copper rivets 4mm' row and read its EUR price from the data — must not hardcode a price; (3) currency_convert with from EUR to USD using the extracted amount; (4) append_row last with [name, EUR price, USD price] referencing extracted/converted values; (5) clear data dependency chain stated. General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "agentic-tool-use-010", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- list_vms(filter: str) -> [{vm_id, name, cpu_util, mem_util, uptime_days}]\n- restart_vm(vm_id: str) -> status\n- snapshot_vm(vm_id: str, label: str) -> snapshot_id\n- delete_vm(vm_id: str) -> status\n\nTask: The VM named 'batch-worker-3' is unhealthy and needs a restart. Safety policy: always snapshot a VM before restarting it. Carry this out.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) list_vms with a name filter to resolve 'batch-worker-3' to a vm_id — must NOT guess the ID; (2) snapshot_vm BEFORE restart_vm (safety policy ordering is the crux); (3) restart only after snapshot succeeds, and should check the snapshot status/handle failure by aborting; (4) delete_vm must NOT be called; (5) restart targets the resolved vm_id. General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "agentic-tool-use-011", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- get_expense(expense_id: str) -> {amount, category, receipt_url, submitter, status}\n- policy_lookup(category: str) -> {limit, requires_receipt_over}\n- approve_expense(expense_id: str, approver: str) -> status\n- reject_expense(expense_id: str, reason: str) -> status\n\nTask: Process expense exp_9021 submitted to approver 'd.ford'. Approve it only if the amount is within the category limit AND (a receipt is attached when the amount exceeds the receipt threshold). Otherwise reject with the specific reason.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) get_expense and policy_lookup (category from the expense, so policy_lookup depends on get_expense — parallel calls only acceptable if category assumed known; best answer sequences them); (2) explicit decision logic combining BOTH conditions: amount <= limit, and receipt present if amount > requires_receipt_over; (3) approve vs reject with approver 'd.ford' / specific reason; (4) must not approve unconditionally; (5) the stated reason must reference which check failed. General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "agentic-tool-use-012", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- create_user(email: str, name: str, role: str) -> user_id\n- assign_license(user_id: str, product: str) -> status\n- add_to_group(user_id: str, group: str) -> status\n- send_welcome_email(user_id: str, template: str) -> status\n- check_license_pool(product: str) -> seats_remaining\n\nTask: Onboard new analyst Mina Park (mina.park@corvid.example) with the 'analyst' role: she needs an 'Insights' product license and membership in the 'analytics' group, then the standard welcome email. If no Insights seats remain, stop before creating the user and report the blocker.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) check_license_pool('Insights') FIRST; if seats_remaining is 0, stop and report — user creation must NOT happen without a seat (this ordering is the crux); (2) create_user with the given email/name/role; (3) assign_license and add_to_group depend on the returned user_id (may be parallel after creation); (4) send_welcome_email last; (5) template 'standard' or equivalent. General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "agentic-tool-use-013", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- monitor_status() -> [{alert_id, service, severity, first_seen}]\n- acknowledge_alert(alert_id: str) -> status\n- escalate(alert_id: str, to: str, note: str) -> escalation_id\n- silence_alert(alert_id: str, minutes: int) -> status\n\nTask: Triage the current alerts: acknowledge every severity-1 alert, escalate any severity-1 alert that has been firing for more than 30 minutes to the 'sre-leads' group, and silence nothing.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) monitor_status first; (2) actions driven by returned data: acknowledge exactly the severity-1 alerts (not all alerts); (3) escalate only the subset whose first_seen is > 30 minutes ago, to 'sre-leads'; (4) silence_alert must NOT be called (explicit instruction); (5) acknowledge should precede/ accompany escalation sensibly; no invented alert_ids. General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
{"id": "agentic-tool-use-014", "clusterId": "agentic-tool-use", "prompt": [{"role": "user", "content": "You are an agent with access to these tools (invented API):\n\n- draft_post(title: str, body: str, tags: [str]) -> draft_id\n- schedule_post(draft_id: str, publish_at: str) -> status\n- get_analytics(post_id: str) -> {views, clicks}\n- list_scheduled() -> [{draft_id, publish_at, title}]\n\nTask: There's a scheduled post titled 'Autumn Launch Teaser'. Move its publish time to 2026-10-01T09:00:00Z, and confirm by listing the schedule afterward.\n\nRespond with the exact sequence of tool calls you would make (name + arguments), noting dependencies between steps and how you handle failures."}], "scoring": {"kind": "llm-judge", "rubric": "Score the assistant's plan/tool-call sequence. (1) list_scheduled first to find the draft_id by title — must not fabricate a draft_id; (2) schedule_post with that draft_id and publish_at '2026-10-01T09:00:00Z'; (3) list_scheduled again AFTER rescheduling to verify (the task explicitly asks to confirm); (4) get_analytics and draft_post are irrelevant and should not be called; (5) ordering: verify call must come after the mutation. General scale: 10 = correct tools in correct order with correct arguments, sensible parallelization, explicit error handling, no unnecessary calls; 7-9 = correct overall plan with minor argument/sequencing slips; 4-6 = right tools but flawed order or missing a required step; 1-3 = wrong tools or ignores the tool interface; 0 = no actionable plan.", "judgeModel": "judge-class", "scale": [0, 10]}}
